{{also|-ický|-ičký}}
==English==

===Etymology===
1935, [[American English]]; from ''[[icky-boo]]'' (circa 1920): [[sickly]], [[nauseated]]; [[baby talk]] corruption of '''[[sick]]'''; from a sentimental term for [[jazz]] music.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɪki/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-icky.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪki|s=2}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er}}

# {{lb|en|informal}} {{U|unpleasantly}} [[sticky]]; [[yucky]]; [[disgusting]].
#: {{ux|en|I stepped in something '''icky''' and it smells terrible.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Wayne Drash|title=On crippled cruise ship, icky jobs fell to ‘amazing’ crew|magazine=w:CNN|url=https://www.cnn.com/2013/02/15/travel/triumph-cruise-crew|date=2013-02-15|passage=Crew member Sachin Sharma of India said workers had no problems with being thrust into the '''icky''' job of handling the red bags filled with human waste.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} {{U|excessively}} [[sentimental]].
# {{lb|en|informal}} {{U|unwell}} or [[upset]]; in a bad state of mind or health.
#: {{ux|en|I'm not going to work today. I just feel '''icky'''.}}

